# GrowthForge — Caddy reverse-proxy / static-file config for the dedicated
# GrowthForge Lightsail instance (ca-central-1). Same pattern as
# dev.quantivarian.com: a Basic-Auth-gated, noindexed dev host serving the
# full app, and a public production host that shows a static "Coming Soon"
# page until launch is approved.
#
# Unlike Quantivarian's current (pre-launch) setup, dev and prod here run as
# TWO SEPARATE api-server processes against two separate build directories
# (/home/ubuntu/dev/app vs /home/ubuntu/prod/app) — GrowthForge's active
# video-rendering work needs dev and prod to stay independently deployable
# even after launch, not just until launch day.
#
# Install: sudo cp Caddyfile /etc/caddy/Caddyfile && sudo systemctl reload caddy

# --- DEVELOPMENT (internal only): full app, hidden from search. ---
# NOTE: an earlier revision also put HTTP Basic Auth in front of this host as
# a network-level lock on top of the app-level gate below. That was removed —
# native browser Basic Auth prompts fire per-resource (main document, XHRs to
# /api/*, etc.) and can fire multiple times in quick succession before the
# browser's credential cache catches up, which produced confusing, unreliable
# stacked login dialogs across OAuth redirects. Access control here now relies
# entirely on the app layer, which does not have that failure mode:
#   - DevAccessGate (frontend): blocks every route until Supabase-authenticated
#     AND admin-approved (`users.canAccessDev = true`)
#   - requireDevAccess (api-server, APP_ENV=development): blocks every /api/*
#     route except sign-in/provisioning unless `canAccessDev = true`
#   - X-Robots-Tag + robots.txt below: keeps it out of search indexes
# If a network-level lock is wanted again later, prefer an IP allowlist for
# known developer IPs over Basic Auth, since it has no client-visible prompt.
dev.usegrowthforge.com {
	route {
		header X-Robots-Tag "noindex, nofollow"
		handle /robots.txt {
			root * /home/ubuntu/dev-static
			file_server
		}
		handle /api/* {
			reverse_proxy localhost:4001
		}
		handle {
			root * /home/ubuntu/dev/app/artifacts/growthforge/dist/public
			try_files {path} /index.html
			file_server
		}
	}
	encode gzip
}

# --- PRODUCTION (pre-launch): serve the "Coming Soon" static page only. ---
# No proxy to the API and no serving of app assets here — the real app is
# fully built and verified on dev.usegrowthforge.com above. At launch time,
# comment out this block and the www redirect below it, then uncomment the
# "LAUNCHED" block further down (or see docs/deployment.md §Promotion).
usegrowthforge.com {
	root * /home/ubuntu/coming-soon
	file_server
	encode gzip
}

www.usegrowthforge.com {
	redir https://usegrowthforge.com{uri} permanent
}

# --- LAUNCHED: uncomment this block (and delete the two blocks above it)
#     once dev has been fully tested and launch is approved. Points at the
#     prod api-server process + the prod build directory — a separate,
#     independently-promoted deployment from dev, not the same process. ---
